1405:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/tcp_in.c ****       /* Trimming the first edge is done by pushing the payload
1406:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/tcp_in.c ****          pointer in the pbuf downwards. This is somewhat tricky since
1407:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/tcp_in.c ****          we do not want to discard the full contents of the pbuf up to
1408:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/tcp_in.c ****          the new starting point of the data since we have to keep the
1409:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/tcp_in.c ****          TCP header which is present in the first pbuf in the chain.
1410:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/tcp_in.c **** 
1411:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/tcp_in.c ****          What is done is really quite a nasty hack: the first pbuf in
1412:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/tcp_in.c ****          the pbuf chain is pointed to by inseg.p. Since we need to be
1413:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/tcp_in.c ****          able to deallocate the whole pbuf, we cannot change this
1414:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/tcp_in.c ****          inseg.p pointer to point to any of the later pbufs in the
1415:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/tcp_in.c ****          chain. Instead, we point the ->payload pointer in the first
1416:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/tcp_in.c ****          pbuf to data in one of the later pbufs. We also set the
1417:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/tcp_in.c ****          inseg.data pointer to point to the right place. This way, the
1418:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/tcp_in.c ****          ->p pointer will still point to the first pbuf, but the
1419:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/tcp_in.c ****          ->p->payload pointer will point to data in another pbuf.
1420:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/tcp_in.c **** 
1421:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/tcp_in.c ****          After we are done with adjusting the pbuf pointers we must
1422:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/tcp_in.c ****          adjust the ->data pointer in the seg and the segment
1423:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/tcp_in.c ****          length.*/
